  <section class="rte-section" aria-labelledby="how-to-use">
    <h2 id="how-to-use">How to Use This Estimator — Step by Step</h2>
    <ol class="rte-steps-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Step 1 — Enter your annual gross Social Security income.</strong> Enter your total annual Social Security benefit before Medicare premium deductions. You can find this on your annual Social Security Benefit Statement (Form SSA-1099), which is mailed every January.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Step 2 — Enter IRA and 401(k) withdrawals.</strong> Include all pre-tax IRA distributions, 401(k) withdrawals, and Required Minimum Distributions (RMDs) you took or plan to take this year. Do not include Roth IRA withdrawals — those are typically tax-free and do not count.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Step 3 — Enter pension, interest, and dividend income.</strong> Include fully taxable pension payments, taxable interest from savings accounts, and ordinary dividends shown on year-end 1099-INT and 1099-DIV forms from your bank and brokerage.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Step 4 — Select your filing status and confirm you are 65 or older.</strong> Choose "Single" or "Married Filing Jointly." Checking the 65+ box adds the IRS senior additional standard deduction — $2,000 for single filers, $1,600 per eligible spouse for joint filers — which directly reduces your taxable income.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="rte-pro-tip">
      <p class="rte-pro-tip-label">💡 Pro Tip</p>
      <p class="rte-pro-tip-text">To stop unexpected year-end tax bills, file IRS Form W-4V with the Social Security Administration to request 7%, 10%, 12%, or 22% voluntary withholding on your monthly Social Security check. Download Form W-4V at <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/fw4v.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Form W-4V (PDF)</a>. For IRA and pension income, request withholding using IRS Form W-4P with your custodian.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Plain Language Explanation Section -->
    <div class="rte-explanation">
      <h3>How the IRS Taxes Social Security — The Provisional Income Formula Explained</h3>
      <p>
        The Provisional Income formula is the IRS's method for determining whether your Social Security benefits are subject to federal income tax. Many retirees don't realize their benefits can be partially taxed until they receive an unexpected tax bill.
      </p>
      <div class="rte-formula-box">
        <strong>Provisional Income = Adjusted Gross Income (AGI) + Tax-Exempt Interest + 50% of Social Security Benefits</strong>
      </div>
      <p>Once calculated, your Provisional Income is compared to IRS thresholds:</p>

      <div class="rte-table-wrap">
        <table class="rte-threshold-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Provisional Income (Single)</th>
              <th>Provisional Income (Joint)</th>
              <th>Social Security Taxable Portion</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Below $25,000</td>
              <td>Below $32,000</td>
              <td>0% — none taxable</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="rte-row-alt">
              <td>$25,000 – $34,000</td>
              <td>$32,000 – $44,000</td>
              <td>Up to 50% taxable</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Above $34,000</td>
              <td>Above $44,000</td>
              <td>Up to 85% taxable</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Note: These thresholds have never been adjusted for inflation since they were set in 1984 and 1993, which means more and more retirees cross them every year as Social Security benefits increase with cost-of-living adjustments (COLA).</p>
    </div>
  </section>

